From 49e29836397917452204adb15945520b7cc502aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 16:24:41 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 78/78] inline-3d(#103): adopt the brokered connection in the
 GPU process
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Branch B of the design of record, half two: the sandboxed side. 0077-B taught the
browser to hand out a connection; this teaches the GPU process to take one, put
its weave session back on it, and hand the new service a GPU-side world it has
never seen before but can make sense of.

THE LADDER, ON THE SEQUENCE THAT SUBMITS

Every step runs on the GPU-MAIN sequence — the one that owns the D3D device, the
weaver_ remote, and the client's State(). No worker thread and no blocking wait
anywhere: the only asynchrony is the mojo hop, whose reply is marshalled back
here exactly as OnWeaveReply's already is. That is a design constraint, not a
convenience — State() is single-sequence per process and the reconnect rebuilds
it, so a worker thread would reintroduce the cross-thread hazard 0071's swap
comment exists to rule out. Cost is a ~10-20 ms hitch on one frame, at most once
per rung, on a frame that was already lost.

Rungs come from DisplayXRWeaveClient::ReconnectDelaySeconds — the SAME 2/5/15/60/
300s ladder 0071 uses and 0077-B exposed, so there is one set of numbers in the
codebase rather than two that can drift. The first rung must not go below 2s:
displayxr-service takes ~2000 ms to exit for relaunch and the runtime client's
own connect ladder already covers landing inside that window.

ADOPT, THEN CREATE — AND DESTROY BEFORE BOTH

DisplayXRWeaveClient::AdoptAndReconnect is destroy, adopt, create:

  * DESTROY is mandatory, not tidy. E0's second probe answered
    XR_ERROR_LIMIT_REACHED — the runtime's one-live-instance-per-process guard —
    so an overlapping create cannot work even where we would prefer the safety.
  * ADOPT goes through the runtime DLL's EXPORTED
    ipc_client_connection_adopt_handle, resolved with GetModuleHandle +
    GetProcAddress on DisplayXRClient.dll, which the Khronos loader already
    mapped into this process pre-sandbox. Never LoadLibrary — that would be a
    genuinely new privileged act from a Low-integrity process — and never the
    DXR_IPC_HANDLE environment form the same runtime patch also accepts: that
    DLL's static-CRT environment snapshot was taken at load time, so a
    SetEnvironmentVariable now is invisible to the getenv that reads it. Same
    trap XRT_FORCE_MODE documents. The env form serves its Android twin, where
    the value exists before the process starts.
  * CREATE consumes the adopted endpoint instead of dialling the pipe, which is
    the entire point: after LowerToken() this process cannot dial.

The handle is duplicated by the runtime, so the caller still owns its copy — and
a ScopedHandle owns it from the moment mojo hands it over to the end of the
reply, on every path. A flapping service must not leak one pipe INSTANCE per
attempt: the service's count is finite and exhausting it would deny service to
every DisplayXR client on the box, not just this browser.

THE SWAP-OVER (§6.3/§6.4)

  * shared_input_ and overlay_input_ are force-REALLOCATED through the existing
    ResetInput + EnsureInput. Both are keyed-mutex textures, and a service that
    died holding the mutex leaves it ABANDONED — the next AcquireSync answers
    WAIT_ABANDONED, which today means zero copies for the rest of the process's
    life. Reallocating beats special-casing WAIT_ABANDONED because it is also
    correct for the other half: the new service has never seen these textures.
  * batch_last_copied_ needs no explicit clear — it is cleared WHOLESALE when
    shared_input_.input_tex is reallocated (the header's contract, enforced by
    the identity check in EnsureBatchInputAndAcquire). "Keep previous" must never
    claim pixels the new service has never been shown.
  * results_ is closed via CloseResultHandles. The new session's runtime-side
    `exported` flag starts clear, so the first post-reconnect submit re-exports
    and Viz re-imports through the existing first-submit/resize path. Keeping the
    old handles would show a FROZEN 3D frame, which is worse than black because
    it looks like it is working.
  * Sticky state is re-asserted verbatim: snap reset to INT32_MIN (the new DP has
    never been told where this window is), latched_screen_flat re-sent (a lost
    latch is a PERMANENTLY ghosted popup over live 3D), requested_3d re-asserted,
    PFNs / ref_space / weave spec re-resolved from the fresh enumeration.

RESET BY SURVIVAL, NOT BY CONNECTING (§6.8)

The ladder resets only after the new session has survived a 60 s stability window
of successful submits. A service crash-looping every three seconds would
otherwise hand out a connection each time, reset the ladder each time, and turn
an outage into a reconnect storm. Losing the session inside that window logs
`flapping` with the generation and the lifetime — the two numbers triage wants —
and explicitly does NOT reset. The generation lives in the client, so both
processes' lines quote one number instead of two that can disagree.

VERSION SKEW AND A RUNTIME THAT CANNOT BROKER

kVersionSkew: one loud unthrottled line naming the remedy, then straight to the
5-minute tail. Never permanent. kUnsupported (a runtime predating spec v9):
terminal for the browser session, ladder stopped, said once — and said honestly,
because SCENES still recover on that runtime (they read the browser's session,
which needs no broker); only the GPU's TILES are stuck mono.

THE renegotiable FLIP (§6.5 bonus)

0071 could only re-negotiate a degraded rig in the browser process, because
re-negotiating meant creating another instance. Brokering removes that
constraint, so a GPU session that came up degraded now climbs the same ladder —
on the 5-minute tail, deliberately, because this side cannot run 0071's
probe-before-swap and every rig attempt therefore tears down a working weave.
|renegotiable| itself keeps its exact meaning ("can create an instance
unaided", still false here) rather than being redefined; the GPU's equivalent is
this rung.

A pipe disconnect while a request is outstanding drops the reply callback, so the
Bind() disconnect handler now also unwedges kInFlight — otherwise a browser-side
hiccup would leave the ladder silent for the process lifetime.

0070's mono recovery draw is untouched, and keeps running for the whole outage
because 0075's latch keeps BatchMode() true. InitOutcome::kUnavailable still
keeps everything dormant: no ladder, no timers, no logs.

Files: gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.{h,cc}, browser/displayxr_weave_client.{h,cc}.
Zero upstream-Chromium files.
